# Normalise line endings in the repository, so that Windows git and WSL git
# agree on what has changed.
#
# Before this file the worktree held a mix of CRLF and LF while the index held
# LF throughout, and the two sides read that differently: Windows git has
# core.autocrlf=true (which normalises on read and so hides it), while WSL git
# leaves it unset and so compared CRLF content against LF blobs. The result was
# ~42 files reported permanently dirty from WSL with a phantom 7,370-line diff
# that vanished under `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol` - which meant a real edit
# hid in the noise for anything reading the tree over /mnt/c.
* text=auto

# The batch launchers are the exception and must stay CRLF: cmd.exe mis-parses
# an LF-only batch file (labels and goto), and oilwatch_env.bat is the shared
# file both unattended launchers call.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# The WSL helper launchers are the other exception, and must stay LF. They are
# shell scripts read by WSL out of a checkout made on Windows, and a CRLF
# shebang is not a shebang - the installed file then fails to exec with an error
# that reads like a missing file. Pinned here because `* text=auto` with this
# repo's core.autocrlf=true would convert to CRLF on checkout for any type it
# does not name, and the worktree copy is what install-wsl-helpers.sh installs.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The icon is binary and Windows reads its bytes as drawn, so it is named here
# rather than left to `* text=auto` to judge.
*.ico binary
